        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "e661b321-ea6a-52d6-bb1a-b5b4199eac51",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Margot Lee Shetterly, Katherine Johnson, Dorothy Vaughan, Mary Jackson\n- Books: Hidden Figures: The American Dream and the Untold Story of the Black Women Who Helped Win the Space Race\n- Organizations: NACA, NASA, Langley Research Center\n- Roles: Human computers, mathematicians, acting supervisor\n\nFacts:\n- Dorothy Johnson Vaughan was born on September 20, 1910, and died on November 10, 2008.\n- In 1949, Dorothy Vaughan became acting supervisor of the West Area Computers and was the first African-American woman to supervise a group of staff at Langley Research Center.\n- Dorothy Vaughan was an African American mathematician and human computer who worked for NACA and NASA at Langley Research Center in Hampton, Virginia.\n- In 2016, Margot Lee Shetterly wrote the non-fiction book Hidden Figures: The American Dream and the Untold Story of the Black Women Who Helped Win the Space Race.\n- The book Hidden Figures follows the lives of human computers including Katherine Johnson, Dorothy Vaughan, and Mary Jackson, who were mathematicians.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
